<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<section xml:id="chapter4_the-modulus-operator">
  <title>The Remainder Operator</title>

  <p>
    The remainder operator works on integer operands and yields the
    <em>remainder</em> when the first operand is divided by the second.
    In C++, the remainder operator is a percent sign, <c>%</c>.
    It is also commonly called the modulus or modulo operator.
  </p>

  <listing label="mod_operator_AC_1">
    <title>
      This program shows the difference between integer division
      and the remainder operator.
    </title>
    <program interactive="activecode" line-numbers="yes" label="mod_operator_AC_1_editor">
      <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int quotient = 7 / 3;
    int remainder = 7 % 3;

    std::cout << quotient << '\n';
    std::cout << remainder << '\n';

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>
  </listing>

  <p>
    Integer division yields 2, while the remainder operator yields 1.
    Thus, 7 divided by 3 is 2 with 1 left over.
  </p>

  <p>
    The remainder operator is useful for checking divisibility.
    If <c>x % y</c> is zero, then <c>x</c> is divisible by <c>y</c>,
    provided that <c>y</c> is not zero.
  </p>

  <p>
    For nonnegative integers, the remainder operator can also be used
    to extract the rightmost digit or digits of a number.
    For example, <c>x % 10</c> yields the rightmost decimal digit of
    <c>x</c>. Similarly, <c>x % 100</c> yields the last two decimal digits.
  </p>

  <exercise label="mod_operator_1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which expression correctly tests whether the integer variable
        <c>x</c> is odd?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x % 2 == 0</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            A remainder of zero when dividing by 2 indicates an even number.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><c>x % 2 != 0</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            An odd integer has a nonzero remainder when divided by 2.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x / 2 == 0</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Integer division gives a quotient, not the remainder needed
            to determine whether a number is odd.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x / 2 != 0</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Integer division gives a quotient, not the remainder needed
            to determine whether a number is odd.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="mod_operator_2">
    <statement>
      <p>Match each remainder expression to its result.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback>
      <p>
        Remember that <c>%</c> gives the remainder after integer division.
      </p>
    </feedback>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise order="1">3 % 2</premise>
        <response>1</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="2">2 % 3</premise>
        <response>2</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="3">6 % 2</premise>
        <response>0</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="4">9 % 6</premise>
        <response>3</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>
</section>
